---
import Workshop from '../../layouts/Workshop.astro';

const prepare = [
  {
    term: 'Evidence to hand',
    text: 'An alternator enquiry and the original label, a legible photo or the technician’s reading.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Callback record',
    text: 'A record of what distinguishes the required unit, or a specific detail to ask the technician for.',
  },
];
---

<Workshop
  title="An alternator fit check | Parts Relay"
  description="Record the markings on an alternator and the details you still need from the workshop before quoting."
  heading="An alternator fit check"
  audience="For distributors. Self-guided, about 15 minutes."
  poster="/assets/alternator.svg"
  posterAlt="Alternator with its connector and pulley"
  prepare={prepare}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    When two units are listed for the same vehicle, the last few characters on the old alternator
    can decide which one to price. Use the worksheet to keep the label reading separate from your
    interpretation.
  </Fragment>
  <section class="workshop-materials">
    <h2>Keep a record of the label</h2>
    <p>
      Download the callback sheet before contacting the workshop. Leave room to record the
      supplier’s question exactly as they ask it.
    </p>
    <div>
      <a
        href="/worksheets/parts-relay-fit-check-worksheet.md"
        download="Parts-Relay-fit-check-worksheet.md"
      >
        Download worksheet ↓
      </a>
      <a href="/journal/alternator-labels/">Read the alternator label guide</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="workshop-agenda">
    <h2>When the suffix is unreadable</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the marking the technician can see. The output rating might still be clear even
      when the end of the manufacturer’s reference has rubbed away. Keep both pieces of information.
    </p>
    <h2>Work through the reference</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <h3>Take the markings in order</h3>
        <p>
          Write the vehicle identifier on the sheet. Record the manufacturer’s part number character
          by character and put the amp rating on a separate line.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Mark the gap</h3>
        <p>
          Note where a character is unreadable. Ask for a closer photo if it would settle the
          reading. Leave a genuine gap open rather than supplying a likely letter from the
          catalogue.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Ask the supplier what separates the units</h3>
        <p>
          If the partial reference leaves more than one candidate, find out which visible detail
          would decide between them. Give the technician that question before they go back to the
          car.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h2>What belongs in your callback record</h2>
    <dl class="workshop-answer">
      <div>
        <dt>Readable markings</dt>
        <dd>Copy only the characters the workshop can verify. Keep units with the rating.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Unresolved characters</dt>
        <dd>Mark whether the gap is at the start, in the middle or at the end of the reference.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Next check</dt>
        <dd>Write the supplier’s exact question about the label, connector or equipment.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Outcome</dt>
        <dd>
          Record which reference the supplier confirmed, or who will call back with the missing
          detail.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <h2>Before you release the quote</h2>
    <p>
      Could a colleague point to the evidence for the quoted reference? When two variants still fit
      the information on hand, keep the quote open until the supplier can distinguish them.
    </p>
    <h2>Using the check on a starter</h2>
    <p>
      Use the same sheet for a starter motor. Replace the alternator rating with the markings the
      supplier needs for that starter application.
    </p>
  </section>
</Workshop>
